WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.040
AI is eating the physical world.

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.880
Building the infrastructure to power the
global artificial intelligence boom

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.800
requires vast amounts of steel, concrete,
and land.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.000
But right now, this global buildout is
hitting a wall.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:14.800
In the U.S.

6
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.280
alone, local communities, worried about
water scarcity and over-taxed electrical

7
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.680
grids, have pushed back, freezing well
over $100 billion in

8
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.680
planned data center investments.

9
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.640
To get that stalled capital moving again,
the industry is

10
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.040
pointing to sustainability frameworks.

11
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.280
The World Economic Forum estimates better
environmental and community practices

12
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.040
could push up to $1 trillion in planned AI
infrastructure

13
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.720
past local resistance.

14
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.240
In this investment risk framework, a local
community rejecting a

15
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.040
development and a cooling system running
out of water are

16
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.920
treated as similar impediments to
construction.

17
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.320
Sustainability becomes the mechanism to
manage those constraints, placate local

18
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.200
opposition, and keep the concrete pouring.

19
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.600
The older idea of sustainable development
included a sense of

20
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.280
limits. But when sustainability is reduced
to a project management

21
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.400
tool, it stops acting as a break on
consumption.

22
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.160
It becomes the lubricant to ensure
development proceeds.

23
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.040
The first line of defense in data center
sustainability is

24
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.680
usually an engineering metric called power
usage effectiveness, or PUE.

25
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.200
It measures exactly how much electricity
entering a facility actually

26
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.480
reaches the computing equipment, rather
than being lost to overheads

27
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.720
like cooling. But efficient hardware is
only half the equation.

28
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.640
Generating an image, reasoning through a
complex prompt, or creating

29
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.840
video consumes hundreds of times more
energy per query than

30
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.280
a simple text search.

31
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.800
As computing becomes cheaper and more
efficient, the industry doesn't

32
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.120
do the same amount of work with less
power.

33
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.680
Instead, they invent heavier workloads,
deploy more autonomous agents, and

34
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.360
generate an exponentially larger volume of
data tokens across the

35
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.000
globe. This chart shows the tension
clearly.

36
00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.000
While the energy required per query, the
shallow downward curve,

37
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.960
continues to improve, total global
electricity consumption from AI data

38
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.400
centers is projected to aggressively
multiply by the end of

39
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.040
the decade. Calculating how efficiently a
resource is converted into

40
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.880
computation means very little if the total
volume of computation

41
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.160
expands far faster than those efficiency
gains.

42
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.400
In the end, mere efficiency is simply not
sufficiency.

43
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.880
To see how this plays out physically, look
at the

44
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.480
island of Tasmania.

45
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.280
Currently, its electrical grid is in
balance.

46
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.800
The state generates roughly as much
electricity as its customers

47
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.040
use. Now, a single company is proposing
hundreds of megawatts

48
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.200
of AI infrastructure in the state.

49
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.760
At full build, these facilities would
consume around 3.3 terawatt

50
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.280
hours of electricity every year, requiring
a severe adjustment to

51
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.160
the island's energy equation.

52
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.200
To offset this, the developer has promised
to support at

53
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.640
least two megawatts of new renewable
generation for every one

54
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.760
megawatt of contracted data center
capacity.

55
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.840
Two for one sounds generous.

56
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.320
But megawatts measure potential capacity,
while actual energy output is

57
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.000
consumed over time.

58
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.600
Because wind and solar fluctuate with the
weather, those large

59
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.360
renewable capacity blocks shrink and vary
in reality, while the

60
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.880
data center load remains a constant,
unyielding drain.

61
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.640
Delivering on that promise means the
specific mix of generation,

62
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.280
the timing of the wind, and the amount of
battery

63
00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.280
firming matter far more than the headline
two-for-one figure.

64
00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.160
A mathematically appealing sustainability
pledge can easily obscure the sheer,

65
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.320
relentless volume of physical energy an AI
facility extracts from

66
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.720
a local grid 24 hours a day.

67
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.360
Generating the power is only the first
step.

68
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:44.920
You also have.

69
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.800
to transport it Concentrating a utility
scale load in a

70
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.640
single location physically alters the
entire electrical grid.

71
00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.880
Accommodating a load of this size requires
heavy physical infrastructure.

72
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.600
It means building new substations,
installing voltage stability mechanisms, and

73
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.920
adding reactive power support.

74
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.280
In one area of Tasmania, network operators
have costed a

75
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.840
credible upgrade option at roughly $370
million.

76
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.440
Regulators in Australia are actively
updating the rules to handle

77
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.400
this. They want to ensure that large data
centers bring

78
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.840
their own renewable generation and pay for
the specific network

79
00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.600
upgrades they cause.

80
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.160
Forcing a corporation to internalize its
physical externalities, making them

81
00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.480
pay the infrastructure costs they create,
is excellent energy policy.

82
00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.080
But while making a project pay its
physical bill successfully

83
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.440
answers how a data center gets built, it
creates the

84
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.920
illusion that the ultimate question has
been settled.

85
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:47.000
Because an electrical grid has physical
limits, allocating power carries

86
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.680
an opportunity cost.

87
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.800
Every megawatt used by a server is a
megawatt denied

88
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.160
to something else.

89
00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.760
You can see this zero-sum reality clearly
when older, heavy

90
00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.280
industry shuts down.

91
00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.040
If a facility like an aluminum or
manganese smelter closes,

92
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.640
hundreds of megawatts of electricity
suddenly become available on the

93
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.000
grid. The state then faces a difficult
choice.

94
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.800
Should this newly available power be sold
to an AI

95
00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.640
data center, used to develop green
hydrogen, or preserved for

96
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.520
a future industry we haven't even invented
yet?

97
00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.160
A highly automated data center might be
willing to pay

98
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.920
premium commercial rates for the
electricity, but after construction is

99
00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.600
finished, it provides far fewer long-term
jobs and local supply

100
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.400
chain benefits than traditional
manufacturing.

101
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.440
Because power is finite, the utility of
artificial intelligence must

102
00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.240
be weighed against every other potential
industry that could have

103
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.840
used that capacity.

104
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.680
The decision always comes down to a single
question: compared

105
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.520
with what? Right now, there is an
institutional blind spot.

106
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.600
Local councils approve the zoning
applications, and grid operators verify

107
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.800
network capacity. But no single authority
conducts a public comparative

108
00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.240
test to decide if an AI facility is
actually the

109
00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.640
most valuable use of that power.

110
00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.200
To manage this, communities need the power
of the veto.

111
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.640
That is, the democratic capacity to reject
a project that

112
00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.160
fully pays its physical costs simply
because it isn't the

113
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.360
preferred use of a finite public resource.

114
00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.040
They also need a dividend, a requirement
that these projects

115
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.520
provide a lasting social return, like
funding local research or

116
00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.360
reserving compute time for universities,
so that some public value

117
00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.040
remains long after the current generation
of hardware becomes obsolete.

118
00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:38.600
Sustainability frameworks, engineering
metrics, and investment risk profiles can tell

119
00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.360
us how efficiently resources are converted
into computation.

120
00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.240
They cannot determine public value.

121
00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.000
We are building an intricate global
machine to ensure AI

122
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.840
pays its physical bill, but we have
completely failed to

123
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.200
decide what our electricity is actually
for.

